  <section>
    <h2>The problem this would solve</h2>
    <p>
      When the tool is asked for the pure-Tamil equivalent of a borrowed word, it answers from a
      community-compiled aggregation of purist word lists. That list works. It also has
      <b> no stated licence</b>, no named editor, and no upstream activity since 2020.
    </p>
    <p>
      Anyone who inspects our sources will find that, and they would be right to think less of the
      tool for it. We grade it D and say so in every answer that rests on it, which is honest and is
      not a solution. A glossary published by the Government of Tamil Nadu is the solution.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where things stand</h2>
    <ol class="asks">
      <li>
        <b>The licence is settled.</b> TVA states in writing that the works of the
        <span class="ta">தமிழ்ப்பெருங்களஞ்சியம்</span> are published under CC BY-SA 4.0, and that licence
        extends to all TVA materials. TVA's stated purpose in hosting them is for others to build on.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>The <span class="ta">கலைச்சொல்</span> glossaries as data are still open.</b> The licence now
        lets us use them wherever we find them. What remains is finding them in a structured form,
        so that each borrowed term is paired with its accepted Tamil equivalent and the attribution
        travels on every answer.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How we use their material</h2>
    <p>
      The TVA degree course books are the teaching material this project's grammar tables were checked
      against. Data derived from TVA material ships with attribution to TVA and stays CC BY-SA, as the
      licence requires. It is never relicensed under the code's Apache-2.0.
    </p>
    <p>
      The books themselves stay out of version control because of their size. The same material is
      mirrored on archive.org, which we read from when tamilvu.org is unavailable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Something we owe them back</h2>
    <p>
      While pinning the classical editions we found that the course books quote Nannūl selectively and
      renumber: their 336, 319 and 136 correspond to <b>337, 320 and 137</b> in the Project Madurai
      edition. Three of our own rule tables carried the course-book numbers and were wrong because of
      it.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is not a complaint. It is the kind of thing that is invisible from inside a syllabus and
      obvious the moment somebody machine-checks every citation, which is a thing this project does
      by accident of its design. The list is theirs if they want it, along with anything else our
      citation tests turn up.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="mailto:thamizh@ief-global.org">thamizh@ief-global.org</a> · we are a nonprofit, the
      code is Apache-2.0, the service is free, and there is no commercial product built on any of
      this.
    </p>
  </section>
